WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.068
Hi, I'm Michelle Klein, member of
the GSB Alumni Association Board, and

00:00:04.068 --> 00:00:06.210
I'm pleased to welcome
you to this production

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.601
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:12.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
change the organization change the world

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:16.669
[MUSIC]

00:00:16.669 --> 00:00:20.926
&gt;&gt; If not you then who?

00:00:20.926 --> 00:00:23.448
If not, you then who?

00:00:23.448 --> 00:00:28.008
You are sitting here,
on the precipice of leaving the GSB

00:00:28.008 --> 00:00:32.950
with the whole world in front of you,
and you can do anything.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.360
And yet some of you are not
sure what your next step is.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.408
Some of you are not sure if
your job is the perfect fit and

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:46.130
some are wondering,
what were these two years about?

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.010
Am I any less confused
than when I started?

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.779
The point of today,

00:00:50.779 --> 00:00:55.915
is to let you know that you are absolutely
prepared for this next step.

00:00:55.915 --> 00:00:59.662
That you all have superpowers,
you all unique and talented and

00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:01.650
you inspire me every single day.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.479
So, we're going to talk
about my life lessons and

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:09.570
I'm going to wrap it around story
you know I love storytelling.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.882
So let's get started,
something you won't believe coz here I am,

00:01:12.882 --> 00:01:16.940
kind of pushy New Yorker telling you
all these things you should be doing.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.030
But I was the shyest kid,
I hid behind my mom.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.550
And it would take a lot for me to speak
up in class or put myself out there.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.090
I grew up with a twin brother, Adam.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.200
Yeah, we were dressed that way.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.550
I have I grew up with
an older sister Jane.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.845
A lot of my students know that she sends
me beautiful clothes are called Jamie

00:01:38.845 --> 00:01:41.139
downs and I'm she protected me as well.

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:46.459
But around age nine, my personality
started to come, and I got a little sassy.

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:53.859
I even made some questionable fashion
choices, see elite I know you're very good

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:57.968
with fashion I don't know what you'd
think of my tartan cape here, but

00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:00.780
it definitely made me stand out.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.728
But through the years,
I came out of my shell,

00:02:03.728 --> 00:02:08.865
I have these life experiences and
these jobs and I got my toolkit.

00:02:08.865 --> 00:02:13.679
And what I've learned is that everything
you experience is something you can

00:02:13.679 --> 00:02:14.428
leverage.

00:02:14.428 --> 00:02:18.655
Even if it's a skill that's dormant and
you haven't used it in a few years,

00:02:18.655 --> 00:02:21.776
you can unearth it and
bring it out at the right moments.

00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:23.940
So here I am now in
front of all of you and

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.065
I'm going to share some of my beliefs and
my principles.

00:02:27.065 --> 00:02:29.730
And these are my principles to live.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.756
I'm going to read it because
I can't memorize them, but

00:02:31.756 --> 00:02:34.210
I'm going to go over today
about leading with love.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.047
When sad or
disappointed helps someone else.

00:02:37.047 --> 00:02:39.235
Compassion over aggression.

00:02:39.235 --> 00:02:42.240
Figure out how to connect to others.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.480
Showing faith in others
leads to their success.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.420
Let people know who you
are by telling your stories.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.733
Listen when people tell you who you are.

00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:55.000
Be accountable own your words and actions.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.314
It's not what happens, it's how you deal
with it all my reputation management

00:02:58.314 --> 00:03:00.590
people know that I believe in that.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:05.074
And then of course, create your own
narrative I must say that 100 times.

00:03:06.845 --> 00:03:11.398
And then finally which I teach
an executive presence, opt in and

00:03:11.398 --> 00:03:15.879
then figure it out, but
don't hold yourself back out of fear.

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:19.990
So here I actually Emily covered this also
I don't really have to say anything but

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.930
I've been here nine years.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.140
And I co created a course reputation
management with JD schrom.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.530
That was my gateway class into here
to my drug addiction to the GSB.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:36.530
And then I created Project You and
strategic pivoting and the two electives.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.820
So today hopefully you will learn from my
past and hopefully it can help the future.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.950
My first lesson is disappointment
can lead to something even better.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.725
When I was at Camp summer camp,
New York City kids got sent away for

00:03:50.725 --> 00:03:54.960
two months to summer camp, because their
parents needed to get them out of the way.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.440
And I was auditioning for Godspell.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.980
I think I was about nine years old and
the summer before I had been Wendy and

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.740
Peter Pan and I killed it.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.320
So I'm like I'm a shoo in for God's spell.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.780
And not only did I not get a roll,
but I didn't even get in the chorus.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.745
So I go back to my cabin and
sit on my bed and

00:04:11.745 --> 00:04:16.650
I'm like feeling so sorry for
myself and feeling misunderstood.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.117
And my counselor comes over and
like Allison,

00:04:19.117 --> 00:04:22.060
the youngest girls in bunk
two are putting on a play.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.950
Why don't you go help them?

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.686
So it's like okay, so
I went down to bunk to and for

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:30.528
the next two weeks I was the director and
the costumer and the person who

00:04:30.528 --> 00:04:34.728
practiced with them and the producer and
these kids put on a wonderful play.

00:04:34.728 --> 00:04:39.558
Through that for some reason I got girl
of the week, and then eventually I got

00:04:39.558 --> 00:04:45.310
colorwork captain and I had a reputation
as being a leader at my summer camp.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.750
If I had gotten to Godspell,
yeah, I might have been good.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.070
But none of that would have happened and

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.676
the lesson wouldn't have been learned for
me.

00:04:52.957 --> 00:04:57.394
I really believe this, that when you
have difficult people in your life,

00:04:57.394 --> 00:05:00.620
it's a challenge but
it's how you handle it.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:04.550
My mom taught me, that first of
all you should lead with love.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.404
But she also told me that when someone
is upset with you or angry or bullying,

00:05:08.404 --> 00:05:11.066
it's because they're coming
from a place of hurt.

00:05:11.066 --> 00:05:13.830
Hurt people, hurt people.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.120
She's like try to think maybe
they're going through a rough time.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.970
Maybe something's happening in
their home life, show compassion.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.945
So I'm sure many of you have been in a
situation where fire meets fire someone's

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:31.294
&gt;&gt; When people are coming at you,
they want that interaction,

00:05:31.294 --> 00:05:34.659
they want to engage you in
a way that can provoke them so

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:37.811
they can let out all that anger and
frustration.

00:05:37.811 --> 00:05:42.716
But I really believe that if you meet fire
with a warm blanket and compassion and

00:05:42.716 --> 00:05:43.830
empathy, well,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:48.758
then you'll there'll be a greater
understanding in you can get things done.

00:05:48.758 --> 00:05:52.268
When you meet someone who's angry with
compassion so you seem really upset,

00:05:52.268 --> 00:05:55.514
can I help in any way or something's
going on what's going on with you.

00:05:55.514 --> 00:05:59.262
You'll see all of a sudden the whole
body language just goes down and

00:05:59.262 --> 00:06:01.180
like this is so frustrating.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:05.880
And then you're like, how can I help and
all of a sudden, there's humanity.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.970
Now, I wish so this is the takeaway,
you can only control your actions.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.620
How many times you've said only they had
said this or they should have done this.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.330
And I always say, yeah,
they should have but they didn't.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.110
So how does that affect you?

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.650
How do you want to show up?

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.190
What's the leader that you want to be?

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.810
How would you handle the situation?

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.997
All you can do is show up,
the way you want to show up and

00:06:26.997 --> 00:06:31.710
role model it and not let someone
else's bad behavior affect you.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.916
Now, it'd be lovely if I took
my own advice but I didn't and

00:06:34.916 --> 00:06:36.403
I had to learn the hard way.

00:06:36.403 --> 00:06:40.334
When I was at the home shopping network,
which was run by Barry Diller and

00:06:40.334 --> 00:06:41.700
Diane Von Furstenberg.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:46.162
I came over from Good Morning America and
my job was to control the home department,

00:06:46.162 --> 00:06:49.300
all the products,
that had to do with garden and kitchen.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.944
And that's a whole another story coz
I don't cook and I don't garden but

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.455
I really am,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:54.250
&gt;&gt; As Bert Alper says

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:56.479
don't let the truth get in
the way of a good story.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.624
So, anyway I went over there to produce
and they matched you up with a producing

00:07:00.624 --> 00:07:03.180
partner, and
they matched me up with this woman Emmy.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.560
Emmy had her own reputation.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.510
She was known as difficult and
sarcastic and kind of a malcontent.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.936
So when she first met me,
she goes Allison, she goes, I'm so

00:07:11.936 --> 00:07:13.160
glad I'm partnered with you.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.320
I hear you're really friendly,
everybody loves you.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.844
I hate people, so
you're going to be my buffer.

00:07:18.844 --> 00:07:21.270
And I'm thinking, wet flag,
I'm in so much trouble.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.858
But you know what, I'm like,
I'm going to make this work.

00:07:23.858 --> 00:07:26.390
Maybe we can balance each other out.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.119
So after two weeks our talent quit.

00:07:29.119 --> 00:07:32.622
And they said, Alison,
you go on air, you sell products.

00:07:32.622 --> 00:07:34.544
And I was like, okay, [LAUGH].

00:07:34.544 --> 00:07:37.496
And so Amy felt betrayed and
abandoned by me.

00:07:37.496 --> 00:07:39.796
And she was so angry at me.

00:07:39.796 --> 00:07:43.285
And she would try to,
not even pass aggressively,

00:07:43.285 --> 00:07:45.489
she just tried to undermine me.

00:07:45.489 --> 00:07:47.952
She turned our whole team against me,
and she made comments about me.

00:07:47.952 --> 00:07:50.874
You're all too young to know
the movie All About Eve.

00:07:50.874 --> 00:07:53.377
But it's about a woman who'll do
anything to get into the movies.

00:07:53.377 --> 00:07:55.253
So she would call me Eve.

00:07:55.253 --> 00:07:59.211
Anyway, I asked her if I could go to
my husband's law school graduation.

00:07:59.211 --> 00:08:01.216
She says, no, you can't go.

00:08:01.216 --> 00:08:02.796
We need you.

00:08:02.796 --> 00:08:03.652
And she said, you're so lucky.

00:08:03.652 --> 00:08:04.814
I'm so understanding always.

00:08:04.814 --> 00:08:06.605
And then I did the wrong thing.

00:08:06.605 --> 00:08:08.091
I escalated.
And I said, no,

00:08:08.091 --> 00:08:10.344
you have been a total bitch.

00:08:10.344 --> 00:08:10.976
God.

00:08:10.976 --> 00:08:12.653
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; First, and

00:08:12.653 --> 00:08:14.582
only time I've used profanity.

00:08:14.582 --> 00:08:15.846
And, boy.

00:08:15.846 --> 00:08:20.682
It just, after that, she would talk in
my ear during live shows, it got so

00:08:20.682 --> 00:08:21.543
much worse.

00:08:21.543 --> 00:08:26.473
And all I realized is that moment
of satisfaction when I got to say

00:08:26.473 --> 00:08:28.036
what I really felt.

00:08:28.036 --> 00:08:29.494
It wasn't worth it.

00:08:29.494 --> 00:08:31.841
I should have been strategic.

00:08:31.841 --> 00:08:35.649
I should have figured out how to
get what I want, and I didn't.

00:08:35.649 --> 00:08:39.672
And so that momentary feeling
of saying those bad words and

00:08:39.672 --> 00:08:42.225
what you feel, it doesn't pay off.

00:08:42.225 --> 00:08:45.710
I lost all control and
it really blew up in my face.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:49.482
So my lesson for
that is take control and power, and

00:08:49.482 --> 00:08:52.745
only I can take care of
how I react to things.

00:08:52.745 --> 00:08:55.961
So you're not a pushover,
though, if you do this.

00:08:55.961 --> 00:09:00.096
You're still a leader because
you're going to show empathy.

00:09:00.096 --> 00:09:03.508
You're going to rely on
facts to make your argument.

00:09:03.508 --> 00:09:06.289
You're going to come up with solutions.

00:09:06.289 --> 00:09:09.021
You're going to set boundaries, hopefully.

00:09:09.021 --> 00:09:11.619
And I don't know if anyone
recognizes this gentleman,

00:09:11.619 --> 00:09:14.005
probably Mary Spicer does
because we're colleagues.

00:09:14.005 --> 00:09:16.478
This was Joel Siegel from
Good Morning America.

00:09:16.478 --> 00:09:19.005
He was a correspondent for movies.

00:09:19.005 --> 00:09:22.552
And when I was an intern at GMA, he used
to take me to free screenings because I

00:09:22.552 --> 00:09:24.694
was such an eager intern, and I loved him.

00:09:24.694 --> 00:09:27.904
When I started working right out
of college, for some reason,

00:09:27.904 --> 00:09:29.549
he likes to haze the new people.

00:09:29.549 --> 00:09:33.921
And he came and he yelled at me in front
of a whole bunch of people and I was so

00:09:33.921 --> 00:09:38.181
humiliated, but I was also really
hurt because I really admired him.

00:09:38.181 --> 00:09:39.206
But I didn't say anything.

00:09:39.206 --> 00:09:42.084
But the next day we were at the mailboxes,
because in those days,

00:09:42.084 --> 00:09:44.690
that's how we communicate
[LAUGH] through mailboxes.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.728
And he comes up, he's like, hey, Alison.

00:09:46.728 --> 00:09:48.007
And I'm like, hi, Joel.

00:09:48.007 --> 00:09:53.803
I said, Joel, I am so
upset with how you talk to me yesterday.

00:09:53.803 --> 00:09:55.845
First of all, it was very unprofessional.

00:09:55.845 --> 00:09:58.064
And second of all, it wasn't warranted.

00:09:58.064 --> 00:10:00.062
And I really like you, and I admire you,
and I love working with you.

00:10:00.062 --> 00:10:07.128
But I can't talk to you right now
because of what you did to me yesterday.

00:10:07.128 --> 00:10:08.995
You could have blown him over.

00:10:08.995 --> 00:10:12.741
I was 21 [LAUGH] but I had boundaries.

00:10:12.741 --> 00:10:15.884
And I knew if I didn't set a precedent,
right, he might do it again.

00:10:15.884 --> 00:10:17.825
And he said, Allison, I'm so sorry.

00:10:17.825 --> 00:10:19.422
He talked like that, Allison.

00:10:19.422 --> 00:10:22.638
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And he's like, I'm so sorry.

00:10:22.638 --> 00:10:26.567
And you know what, he never did it again,
and we were very close.

00:10:26.567 --> 00:10:31.007
What I'm trying to say is that the
exception is if abuse is consistent, and

00:10:31.007 --> 00:10:32.636
then you have to report it.

00:10:32.636 --> 00:10:37.059
But otherwise,
you can tell anybody anything as long

00:10:37.059 --> 00:10:41.986
as you say it in a nice way,
as long as you say it in a calm way.

00:10:41.986 --> 00:10:45.945
So my next tip is connect
to others on a human level.

00:10:45.945 --> 00:10:50.104
When I was at Penn, my first year I took
psychology, it was between psychology and

00:10:50.104 --> 00:10:51.746
TV by the way, just so you know.

00:10:51.746 --> 00:10:55.584
And there was this study
called the bystander effect.

00:10:55.584 --> 00:10:56.744
I'm sure you've heard of it.

00:10:56.744 --> 00:11:00.842
It's where the people, if it's a group of
people and someone's screaming for help,

00:11:00.842 --> 00:11:02.086
and no one does anything.

00:11:02.086 --> 00:11:07.008
There was a very big case called the Kitty
Genovese Case where a woman was stabbed in

00:11:07.008 --> 00:11:10.375
a courtyard and screamed and
no one came out to help her.

00:11:10.375 --> 00:11:14.728
But what the study said is,
if you can single one person out and

00:11:14.728 --> 00:11:20.438
connect to them as a human being, it's
much more likely that they will help you.

00:11:20.438 --> 00:11:24.625
So don't scream like fire on a train,
like go up to someone and say,

00:11:24.625 --> 00:11:25.732
I need your help.

00:11:25.732 --> 00:11:27.687
So I remember this and
I had an experience.

00:11:27.687 --> 00:11:30.698
I was in London my junior year,
I was studying abroad.

00:11:30.698 --> 00:11:34.093
And because I was a city girl,
I love taking the tube.

00:11:34.093 --> 00:11:36.645
I thought it was much better
than the New York City subway.

00:11:36.645 --> 00:11:38.947
And I was coming back from the library and
it was 11:30 at night.

00:11:38.947 --> 00:11:44.083
And I got out on the platform and
it didn't occur to me how late it was.

00:11:44.083 --> 00:11:46.412
And it was deserted.

00:11:46.412 --> 00:11:47.586
And I get out, and

00:11:47.586 --> 00:11:52.608
a group of young men who are obviously
drunk came out of a carriage a few down.

00:11:52.608 --> 00:11:56.551
And they see me and they start
screaming at me and heckling me.

00:11:56.551 --> 00:11:57.730
Hey, Miss America.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:59.538
Hey, Miss America.

00:11:59.538 --> 00:12:00.550
And my heart stopped.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:03.543
I'm like, my god,
I'm going to be in a bad situation.

00:12:03.543 --> 00:12:05.762
Why didn't I anticipate this?

00:12:05.762 --> 00:12:09.369
And I remembered this, and
I started walking towards them.

00:12:09.369 --> 00:12:11.551
And I said, hey,
how'd you know I was American?

00:12:11.551 --> 00:12:14.484
Was it my down jacket and
sneakers that gave it away?

00:12:14.484 --> 00:12:18.829
And then I went right up to the guy
who was obviously the leader.

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:21.347
And I said, hey, can you do me a favor?

00:12:21.347 --> 00:12:23.004
I need someone to walk me home.

00:12:23.004 --> 00:12:27.146
It's late and
you guys look like you can protect me.

00:12:27.146 --> 00:12:28.453
Would you walk me home?

00:12:28.453 --> 00:12:29.831
And they said, sure.

00:12:29.831 --> 00:12:32.403
And I put my hand through this
arm of this guy and I'm like, so

00:12:32.403 --> 00:12:33.521
where are you guys going?

00:12:33.521 --> 00:12:36.439
It looks like you're celebrating.

00:12:36.439 --> 00:12:39.793
My heart was pounding, and
they became my brothers.

00:12:39.793 --> 00:12:41.217
And they walked me home.

00:12:41.217 --> 00:12:42.946
Now-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:42.946 --> 00:12:44.741
&gt;&gt; It was risky.

00:12:44.741 --> 00:12:46.959
And I'm not saying that'll work.

00:12:46.959 --> 00:12:48.781
But that's what I was thinking about.

00:12:48.781 --> 00:12:51.960
I was thinking about connecting and
humanizing, and

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.855
not escalating a situation that
could have gone a different way.

00:12:55.855 --> 00:13:00.595
I really believe that that eye contact,
the humanity that people share,

00:13:00.595 --> 00:13:02.897
can help you in a sticky situation.

00:13:02.897 --> 00:13:06.469
All right [LAUGH] strange picture, right?

00:13:06.469 --> 00:13:09.028
There's definitely a story about this.

00:13:09.028 --> 00:13:11.161
My next tip, show faith in others.

00:13:11.161 --> 00:13:12.737
I'm at Good Morning, America.

00:13:12.737 --> 00:13:15.073
That gentleman over there
is Ron Reagan Junior.

00:13:15.073 --> 00:13:16.473
President Reagan's son.

00:13:16.473 --> 00:13:19.429
And he was a correspondent for GMA.

00:13:19.429 --> 00:13:23.655
And we had to do this really boring
piece on how the lights are changed for

00:13:23.655 --> 00:13:25.351
the Empire State Building.

00:13:25.351 --> 00:13:27.700
And so every holiday, they change it and

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:32.414
we were going to interview the inventor of
the lights in the Billboards in New York.

00:13:32.414 --> 00:13:34.766
And we were like, this is the most
boring piece, that we're stuck.

00:13:34.766 --> 00:13:36.301
So Ron has this great idea.

00:13:36.301 --> 00:13:38.743
He goes, I've got a great idea,
it's Empire State Building.

00:13:38.743 --> 00:13:42.105
You'll be and
we'll get someone in a monkey suit.

00:13:42.105 --> 00:13:45.121
And in every shot,
there'll be King Kong chasing you or

00:13:45.121 --> 00:13:48.859
carrying you while you're flailing,
and it'll be a running joke.

00:13:48.859 --> 00:13:51.066
And we're like, brilliant, hysterical.

00:13:51.066 --> 00:13:54.280
So I had this intern Matt
had a little crush on me.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:55.140
And I threw him a bone.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.349
I'm like, Matt, would you like to be
in the monkey suit for the shoot?

00:13:58.349 --> 00:14:00.427
And he's like, yes, please.

00:14:00.427 --> 00:14:03.422
So we go to the Empire State Building.

00:14:03.422 --> 00:14:06.324
We're in the 88th floor on the balcony.

00:14:06.324 --> 00:14:09.071
Ron's inside with this really boring,
I mean,

00:14:09.071 --> 00:14:11.284
this nice older man interviewing him.

00:14:11.284 --> 00:14:15.965
And I'm in Matt's arms with
my arms around his neck.

00:14:15.965 --> 00:14:18.638
And I'm waiting for
the director to say action.

00:14:18.638 --> 00:14:20.278
And then I do something
I shouldn't have done.

00:14:20.278 --> 00:14:23.993
I said, Matt, whatever you do,
don't drop me.

00:14:23.993 --> 00:14:28.353
And they go action, and
he takes one step, trips, falls,

00:14:28.353 --> 00:14:31.329
drops me on my back and
falls on top of me.

00:14:31.329 --> 00:14:34.199
Monkey cement sandwich, not good.

00:14:34.199 --> 00:14:37.893
I had to do the take 42 more times so
the bad back.

00:14:37.893 --> 00:14:39.578
What I did is I made
this poor guy nervous.

00:14:39.578 --> 00:14:41.715
I didn't show faith in him, right?

00:14:41.715 --> 00:14:45.377
If I had just said,
this is going to be fun, let's do it.

00:14:45.377 --> 00:14:49.451
And I've learned through my own life
that when someone shows doubt to me,

00:14:49.451 --> 00:14:50.379
I doubt myself.

00:14:50.379 --> 00:14:55.021
So as a leader, I try to be empowering and
I try to say, you can do this.

00:14:55.021 --> 00:14:56.731
I believe in you.

00:14:56.731 --> 00:14:59.404
It's the best gift you
can give to somebody.

00:14:59.404 --> 00:15:02.979
Because if you think you doubt them,
they're going to doubt themselves and

00:15:02.979 --> 00:15:05.469
they're not going to perform
to their best ability.

00:15:05.469 --> 00:15:08.937
If you treat me like an idiot,
I'm going to act like one.

00:15:08.937 --> 00:15:12.147
By the way Matt got me a nice
bag of Kiwis to apologize.

00:15:12.147 --> 00:15:13.954
I really liked Kiwis back then.

00:15:13.954 --> 00:15:18.760
So storytelling, how many by show of
hands know that I love storytelling?

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:20.320
Yeah, we all know this.

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.430
&gt;&gt; Storytelling is a weapon that I want
you all to use in your life, okay.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:27.940
And my storytelling skills started early.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:30.613
And I really thought about
why it's such a passion for

00:15:30.613 --> 00:15:32.940
me, and
there are proof points along the way.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:37.574
One, I was a very avid reader growing
up I used to read a book a night.

00:15:37.574 --> 00:15:43.660
And for me, it taught me about dialogues
and emotions and interactions with people.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.233
I mean,
I think that's why I like psychology too.

00:15:46.233 --> 00:15:48.621
But how everything worked together and

00:15:48.621 --> 00:15:51.740
I also was able to visualize
what things look like.

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:54.810
I always preferred books to movies.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.926
And then my mother,
as crazy as it is, in New York City.

00:15:58.926 --> 00:16:03.680
We walk and if a movie had already
started, my mom would take my brother and

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.150
I in halfway through.

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:09.710
I know child abuse right terrible and
we'd sit in the back row and for

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:13.879
an hour I'd have to figure out
what the heck is going on right.

00:16:13.879 --> 00:16:17.459
And then we'd sit when the movie was over
everyone would leave they only had five

00:16:17.459 --> 00:16:20.827
minute intermissions in those days in
the olden days when they had horse and

00:16:20.827 --> 00:16:21.380
carriage.

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:25.209
And we'd stay and
I'd watch the first hour of the movie and

00:16:25.209 --> 00:16:27.173
think did I get that right.

00:16:27.173 --> 00:16:31.801
So what this taught me is I had to figure
out the plot draw inferences look for

00:16:31.801 --> 00:16:33.349
verbal and visual cues.

00:16:33.349 --> 00:16:38.528
It was pretty much strategic
communication right as a youngster.

00:16:38.528 --> 00:16:43.110
And then I eavesdrop, I'm sorry if you've
ever seen me, I'm a big eavesdropper.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:46.172
I like to go and read and eat,
but I would be listening in, and

00:16:46.172 --> 00:16:48.940
I would be trying figuring out,
are you on a date?

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.200
Are you fighting?

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:52.995
And I'm going to watch your
body language and see,

00:16:52.995 --> 00:16:55.440
does that person seem happy or not.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:59.813
So yes, I do it, and if you see me at
Coupa, I'm probably eavesdropping on you.

00:16:59.813 --> 00:17:03.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then okay, we all know this, right.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.640
I'm a pop culture fanatic.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:10.276
I was faded my mother named me after
Allison Mackenzie from the first soap

00:17:10.276 --> 00:17:12.950
opera ever on TV called Peyton Place.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:16.699
So I was forced to be in pop culture and
media and that Mia Farrow,

00:17:16.699 --> 00:17:18.931
she was a great actress at that point.

00:17:18.931 --> 00:17:22.053
And then we know I have some
questionable reading material.

00:17:22.053 --> 00:17:26.705
But during my teenage years I was paid to
read these magazines I had to look for

00:17:26.705 --> 00:17:28.025
gossip and stories.

00:17:28.025 --> 00:17:31.662
And it was important for me to know
who's doing what with whom, don't judge.

00:17:31.662 --> 00:17:35.677
And then I have an interesting talent
where I have read every issue of

00:17:35.677 --> 00:17:38.011
People Magazine since its inception.

00:17:38.011 --> 00:17:41.532
I'm really good at table talk, and
I'm a lifeline for a lot of people and

00:17:41.532 --> 00:17:43.056
who wants to be a millionaire.

00:17:43.056 --> 00:17:47.154
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So my other storytelling technique is I

00:17:47.154 --> 00:17:50.371
love movies and
this was my favorite movie growing up.

00:17:50.371 --> 00:17:54.126
And my best friend, Jamie,
at the time had never seen it, so

00:17:54.126 --> 00:17:58.857
I forced her to watch it at home one night
on cable and then the cable went out.

00:17:58.857 --> 00:18:01.896
So I started acting it out for
her and doing all the dialogues.

00:18:01.896 --> 00:18:03.497
I must have seen it 20 times.

00:18:03.497 --> 00:18:07.420
Then the cable came back on, and
I was way ahead of where the movie was.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.250
So we started to watch it again.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:11.690
And she looks at me
because you are a freak.

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:14.370
She's like,
I feel like I've seen this movie already.

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:16.600
It's crazy, you know, every line.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:18.992
So what it showed was
my recall of dialogue,

00:18:18.992 --> 00:18:21.849
my ability to act out scenes
visual communication.

00:18:21.849 --> 00:18:26.410
And change my voice for each character
some vocal variety shown thrown in.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.900
So there you go again,
strategic communication.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:31.031
When you tell a story,

00:18:31.031 --> 00:18:34.830
this is what you're telling people
I want you to know who I am.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:38.270
I want you to know why I care about what
I care about and why I do what I do.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:43.149
And I want you to know my strengths but
also my quirks.

00:18:43.149 --> 00:18:48.668
And I want you to know what makes me
different from everybody else out there.

00:18:48.668 --> 00:18:52.405
Maybe through my life experience,
maybe through my challenges, and

00:18:52.405 --> 00:18:54.890
my story is going to prove
my credibility to you.

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:56.892
Even though I did something
that makes no sense.

00:18:56.892 --> 00:19:01.133
10 years ago, I learned something from it.

00:19:01.133 --> 00:19:03.497
All right, where's Erin raise your hand?

00:19:03.497 --> 00:19:06.971
Okay, Erin knows the story, Erin was
born the year of the Missouri floods and

00:19:06.971 --> 00:19:09.971
I told the story and
strategic communication she's like soon.

00:19:09.971 --> 00:19:15.493
But really that resonates because I was
born I'm like the stories for you Erin.

00:19:15.493 --> 00:19:20.199
So for those of you haven't heard
it Good Morning America sent me

00:19:20.199 --> 00:19:22.038
to the Missouri floods.

00:19:22.038 --> 00:19:26.491
This wasn't my favorite job was to book
live guests during big events like floods

00:19:26.491 --> 00:19:29.958
but my first day I was in the helicopter
and I'm with the crew and

00:19:29.958 --> 00:19:31.417
we're taking pictures.

00:19:31.417 --> 00:19:35.582
Looking just like this, just the,
the tops of houses on water and

00:19:35.582 --> 00:19:38.330
it was just a very horrifying experience.

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:43.699
To go over this, all this water and then
my job was to go to the Red Cross shelter.

00:19:43.699 --> 00:19:46.297
Find a family and convince them to come

00:19:46.297 --> 00:19:51.830
with our crew back to their house to
rescue pets or personal belongings.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:54.380
So, I went to the Red Cross shelter.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:57.234
I talked to the head of the fire
department, I got a boat.

00:19:57.234 --> 00:20:01.256
And then I had to find a family, and there
was this man standing against a wall and

00:20:01.256 --> 00:20:04.370
his family was sitting on
the ground next to him.

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:07.280
And I went over to him and I said,
hi, I'm from Good Morning America.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:09.270
I have a crew and we have a boat.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.504
Would you like to go
back to your house and

00:20:11.504 --> 00:20:14.674
maybe rescue some of your personal items?

00:20:14.674 --> 00:20:16.806
Well, he started to scream at me.

00:20:16.806 --> 00:20:18.382
He said How dare you.

00:20:18.382 --> 00:20:22.820
You want to exploit me at the worst
time in my life for National TV.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:26.920
I have lost everything, am homeless.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:31.687
You're going to go home in three days
to your warm house but am homeless.

00:20:31.687 --> 00:20:35.680
And I just let him go on and
on and when he was done.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:38.680
I put my hand on his arm and
I said, I am so sorry,

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:42.580
please accept my apology if I've
made this any worse for you.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:47.650
I wish you luck,
God bless you and I walked away.

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:51.477
And about a minute later, I feel this tap
on my shoulder, and it's the man, and

00:20:51.477 --> 00:20:54.350
he's like, so how would it work
with the boat and the crew?

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:59.376
And so my lesson which a lot
of my class has heard already,

00:20:59.376 --> 00:21:02.006
is that storytelling goes both ways.

00:21:02.006 --> 00:21:06.411
He needed to tell me his story and
I needed to listen.

00:21:06.411 --> 00:21:08.880
He needed to get it off his chest and
be heard.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.680
And I needed to understand
where he was coming from.

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:17.430
And when that trust was built, then he
wanted to do and by the way, he went back.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.510
It was a great experience for him.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:21.582
And he did rescue personal belongings.

00:21:21.582 --> 00:21:24.897
And the executive producer called me and
said that was a great segment but

00:21:24.897 --> 00:21:26.660
that wasn't the point.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:30.210
And I always think back to this
is not doing the right thing.

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:33.160
So if I were to do it now or
even a few years after that,

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:36.329
because I think I was only about 22 or
23 at the time.

00:21:36.329 --> 00:21:39.390
I would have said hi, I'm Allison
Kluger I'm from Good Morning America.

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:43.229
I am so sorry for what you're
going through I don't know if this

00:21:43.229 --> 00:21:46.640
will help you at all, but
I have a boat and a crew.

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:49.300
Would you like to try to get
something from your house, and

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:52.380
would you like to show America
what is going on here?

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.130
So we can get more support?

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:59.230
That is how I would have approached it,
but sometimes you have to fumble, right.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:01.448
To learn the right way to do something and

00:22:01.448 --> 00:22:05.670
I always try to say never make
the same mistake twice hopefully.

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:07.060
So it was a big learning lesson.

00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:11.698
Bob Marcus who gets in
reputation management says,

00:22:11.698 --> 00:22:15.381
if I don't know how you've
struggled how can I connect to you?

00:22:15.381 --> 00:22:18.880
So again it's that compassion,
it's asking people questions.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:19.940
Why are you upset?

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:21.300
What can I do?

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:24.870
That's where the connection comes in.

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:27.545
So many of you know I teach with Tyra, and

00:22:27.545 --> 00:22:31.423
we had Gary Vaynerchuk come in
to talk about social media.

00:22:31.423 --> 00:22:33.398
And where to put your story and

00:22:33.398 --> 00:22:38.386
the reassuring news from Gary was that
you don't have to use social media.

00:22:38.386 --> 00:22:42.206
But if you are pick a platform
that works for you.

00:22:42.206 --> 00:22:45.376
Whether it's Instagram and
its visual or Facebook or

00:22:45.376 --> 00:22:49.400
whether you're in service of somebody and
you want to promote them.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:54.220
But he said if you're going to do it,
stick with it and be on top of it.

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:58.056
But it's very important for
you to know where to take your story and

00:22:58.056 --> 00:22:59.300
how to broadcast it.

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:04.170
You also need to know how
long to tell your story.

00:23:04.170 --> 00:23:08.619
So for some people, just 30 seconds
might be enough of your narrative,

00:23:08.619 --> 00:23:11.225
just the high points or
a minute and a half.

00:23:11.225 --> 00:23:14.299
Or three minutes are sometimes five
minutes but if you don't know your

00:23:14.299 --> 00:23:17.268
audience and you tell a really long
story you're going to lose them and

00:23:17.268 --> 00:23:21.490
they're going to be frustrated
This is a really important point.

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:26.448
In my class is personal branding and
strategic pivoting, I make sure that my

00:23:26.448 --> 00:23:31.339
last assignment is that my students
deliver a minute and a half narrative.

00:23:31.339 --> 00:23:35.614
For personal branding, it's who I am, what
I've done and why I'm going to move into

00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:38.747
this personal brand and
how it's going to be of service to you.

00:23:38.747 --> 00:23:42.725
Strategic pivoting is what I've done,
who I am, why I want to pivot and

00:23:42.725 --> 00:23:44.891
how it will benefit you very similar.

00:23:44.891 --> 00:23:49.830
But the exercise is, if you can tell
your narrative in a minute and a half,

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:53.976
someone else can take that story and
carry it somewhere else,

00:23:53.976 --> 00:23:58.780
where someone can be of service to you and
can create a new opportunity.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:03.111
So this project was done in
a TV station before COVID.

00:24:03.111 --> 00:24:07.066
And they'd go on live television and
have to do this narrative,

00:24:07.066 --> 00:24:08.466
and it was broadcast.

00:24:08.466 --> 00:24:10.135
So would you like to hear?

00:24:10.135 --> 00:24:11.134
Let's hear some narratives.

00:24:11.134 --> 00:24:13.659
I know some of my students have seen
this but it's worth seeing it again.

00:24:13.659 --> 00:24:15.729
So this is from Project You.

00:24:15.729 --> 00:24:17.766
[MUSIC]

00:24:17.766 --> 00:24:19.454
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
[MUSIC]

00:24:19.454 --> 00:24:21.456
[LAUGH]

00:24:21.456 --> 00:24:25.657
[MUSIC]

00:24:25.657 --> 00:24:29.975
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Nancy and
I love to live life out loud.

00:24:29.975 --> 00:24:35.222
From the necklaces I love to rock to the
melanin I proudly wear on my skin, color

00:24:35.222 --> 00:24:40.567
and vibrancy is such a huge part of who I
am, but it hasn't always been that way.

00:24:40.567 --> 00:24:45.382
I was born with a disability in my arm and
my spinal cord called Compiz palsy.

00:24:45.382 --> 00:24:49.857
Before I even knew my name, doctors put
limits on what I could achieve, and

00:24:49.857 --> 00:24:53.208
they told my parents,
I can never live independently.

00:24:53.208 --> 00:24:56.595
But I refused to believe that,
and I rebelled.

00:24:56.595 --> 00:24:59.761
I saw how vibrant I could be,
and I grew a passion for

00:24:59.761 --> 00:25:04.126
helping other people see their own
vibrancy, through photography.

00:25:04.126 --> 00:25:07.866
Photography has shown me that I'm so
much bigger than this body, and

00:25:07.866 --> 00:25:11.485
it's allowed me to connect with
people in deep and intimate ways.

00:25:11.485 --> 00:25:15.996
I built my company Akos&amp;Co to share
the stories of women of color,

00:25:15.996 --> 00:25:20.680
it's been my absolute honor to share
their vibrancy with the world.

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:25.312
And it's my big hairy scary
dream to take this company from

00:25:25.312 --> 00:25:29.103
being about photography
to a lifestyle brand.

00:25:29.103 --> 00:25:32.386
Think cookware and interior design, but

00:25:32.386 --> 00:25:37.177
designed with the needs and
wants of a woman in color in mind.

00:25:37.177 --> 00:25:43.524
I want to help other women expose their
true color in every aspect of their lives.

00:25:43.524 --> 00:25:45.791
&gt;&gt; Every time I see this, I'm moved.

00:25:45.791 --> 00:25:47.659
You know so much about Nancy now.

00:25:47.659 --> 00:25:51.189
You know she had a challenge,
you know she loves photography,

00:25:51.189 --> 00:25:53.863
you know she wants to
create a lifestyle brand.

00:25:53.863 --> 00:25:57.564
And there's enough there that
you could seriously just go and

00:25:57.564 --> 00:26:00.565
talk about Nancy to someone else and
promote her.

00:26:00.565 --> 00:26:03.160
She also was the embodiment
of what she said,

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:06.381
with the beautiful colorful
necklace she was wearing.

00:26:06.381 --> 00:26:07.790
When she finished this class,

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:11.215
she took a black and white picture of
everyone in our class and gave it to us.

00:26:11.215 --> 00:26:12.122
And she took that one to me.

00:26:12.122 --> 00:26:15.637
It's one of my favorite headshots
because of course my hair looks awesome.

00:26:15.637 --> 00:26:19.160
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It's all about the hair.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:21.568
All right,
this is from strategic pivoting.

00:26:21.568 --> 00:26:31.815
[MUSIC]

00:26:31.815 --> 00:26:33.049
&gt;&gt; Good evening, Sharks.

00:26:33.049 --> 00:26:35.188
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I really just always wanted to

00:26:35.188 --> 00:26:35.745
say that.

00:26:35.745 --> 00:26:38.195
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Good evening coaches,

00:26:38.195 --> 00:26:42.668
my name is Ross Pomerantz, and I'm in the
process of pivoting from enterprise sales

00:26:42.668 --> 00:26:45.574
into something far more
disappointing to my parents.

00:26:45.574 --> 00:26:48.044
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Comedy writing.

00:26:48.044 --> 00:26:50.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Last week I did a poll for

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:51.413
20 of my closest friends and

00:26:51.413 --> 00:26:54.514
family and I asked them what they
thought my greatest strength was.

00:26:54.514 --> 00:26:57.476
20 out of 20 said humor.

00:26:57.476 --> 00:26:59.357
Now, if that wasn't enough, ten years ago,

00:26:59.357 --> 00:27:01.538
I was voted the fifth-funniest
person in my family.

00:27:01.538 --> 00:27:03.359
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And my brother, my dad,

00:27:03.359 --> 00:27:06.656
my sister stuffed animal Chester,
and our blind pet parakeet.

00:27:06.656 --> 00:27:08.381
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So yes, I've been destined for

00:27:08.381 --> 00:27:10.011
comedic greatness for a long time now.

00:27:10.011 --> 00:27:12.699
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The other strength that popped up was

00:27:12.699 --> 00:27:16.703
discipline, which undoubtedly comes
from my background, playing baseball.

00:27:16.703 --> 00:27:20.218
Where my only regret was I didn't
get a chance to strike that man out.

00:27:20.218 --> 00:27:23.142
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, between humor and discipline,

00:27:23.142 --> 00:27:25.289
I've been put in a very unique position.

00:27:25.289 --> 00:27:29.390
My pilot started seven years ago, when
I started making comedy videos online.

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:33.250
I created an alter ego,
a persona called Corporate Bro,

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:37.685
in parity of Silicon Valley's sales life,
in parity on my life.

00:27:37.685 --> 00:27:39.997
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Fast-forward to today,

00:27:39.997 --> 00:27:42.354
I have nearly 120,000 fans.

00:27:42.354 --> 00:27:45.399
I've gotten millions of views
across all social media platforms.

00:27:45.399 --> 00:27:49.541
And I even get invited to go speak at tech
companies, which is utterly ridiculous.

00:27:49.541 --> 00:27:52.989
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, that isn't where it ends,

00:27:52.989 --> 00:27:54.070
I don't think.

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:57.781
The last year and a half I spent writing
a pilot TV show with my younger brother,

00:27:57.781 --> 00:28:00.535
which frankly, I think Is our best work,
which is rare for

00:28:00.535 --> 00:28:03.703
me to say because frankly I've
hated everything I've ever done.

00:28:03.703 --> 00:28:05.550
So Alex, if you know anyone at WMV,

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:08.384
Allison if you know anyone in
the TV industry at all, or

00:28:08.384 --> 00:28:12.590
Jenny if you know somebody at Google TV,
I would be honored to share it with them.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:16.780
So I'm very excited to take my launch
because I have absolutely no idea where

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:18.029
I'm going to land on.

00:28:18.029 --> 00:28:20.135
Thank you.

00:28:20.135 --> 00:28:21.376
&gt;&gt; Fabulous, right?

00:28:21.376 --> 00:28:22.850
Do you know a lot about Ross?

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:24.759
Did he not role model the humor?

00:28:24.759 --> 00:28:28.583
I mean, you heard, we were a natural
laugh track, Alex, and the author,

00:28:28.583 --> 00:28:30.666
Jenny from pivot and I were hysterical.

00:28:30.666 --> 00:28:35.352
There was so much in there, it was a tad
too long, but he could shorten it, but

00:28:35.352 --> 00:28:39.826
the point was you understood what
the building steps were to this pivot and

00:28:39.826 --> 00:28:40.999
his credibility.

00:28:40.999 --> 00:28:43.154
And he really knows his narrative, and

00:28:43.154 --> 00:28:46.169
he's told me he's used it in
a lot of different forms.

00:28:46.169 --> 00:28:51.481
So people tell you who you are I was a pen
my freshman year in creative writing,

00:28:51.481 --> 00:28:55.671
I love creative writing, but
my teacher Biff Rose hated me.

00:28:55.671 --> 00:29:00.015
And I was always like the goody two shoes
in English because I'm not a Math student,

00:29:00.015 --> 00:29:00.831
as we all know.

00:29:00.831 --> 00:29:04.723
But she hated writing, and I'd always get
low grades, and finally, I found out why.

00:29:04.723 --> 00:29:08.350
And she said, Allison,
you have very complex thoughts, but

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.319
you write like People Magazine.

00:29:10.319 --> 00:29:14.479
I'm not kidding, I had a People Magazine
in my backpack at that time.

00:29:14.479 --> 00:29:17.753
But I did not whip it out and
I was humiliated.

00:29:17.753 --> 00:29:19.087
I felt that I was stupid,

00:29:19.087 --> 00:29:23.229
I felt that I was going to fail and that
she didn't appreciate anything I wrote.

00:29:23.229 --> 00:29:28.469
But she told me what my brand was
at a very young age, messaging for

00:29:28.469 --> 00:29:33.247
the masses, taking complex thoughts and
funneling them.

00:29:33.247 --> 00:29:36.352
So that it's a very top line message.

00:29:36.352 --> 00:29:37.963
And that is what you do in TV.

00:29:37.963 --> 00:29:39.281
That is what you do in coaching.

00:29:39.281 --> 00:29:40.576
And that's when you do in education.

00:29:40.576 --> 00:29:44.684
So Biff did me a favor,
I just didn't listen hard enough, and

00:29:44.684 --> 00:29:47.528
I was too young to appreciate that wisdom.

00:29:47.528 --> 00:29:50.238
But I go back to it a lot, and think, wow,

00:29:50.238 --> 00:29:53.272
I think my superpower
showed up really early.

00:29:53.272 --> 00:29:56.724
So think about what
someone says about you.

00:29:56.724 --> 00:30:00.729
You're so good at this or
I wish I could do this the way you did it.

00:30:00.729 --> 00:30:03.708
Whatever it is,
think about what people tell you,

00:30:03.708 --> 00:30:05.657
because they see how you land on them.

00:30:05.657 --> 00:30:10.788
And they might be able to encourage you
in ways, you can't do for yourself.

00:30:10.788 --> 00:30:14.338
So this is my son Logan,
he was about eight in this picture.

00:30:14.338 --> 00:30:17.036
And this story is really
about when he was four,

00:30:17.036 --> 00:30:19.267
have you heard of Bing Nursery School?

00:30:19.267 --> 00:30:20.665
Think here at Stanford, okay?

00:30:20.665 --> 00:30:24.756
So getting my son's into Bing was
harder than getting into the GSB.

00:30:24.756 --> 00:30:27.730
I'm not kidding,
I wrote it a kick-ass letter and

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:31.479
I got them in because I really felt
to be the best school for them.

00:30:31.479 --> 00:30:36.261
And I was at orientation and when they
were talking about half acre of playroom

00:30:36.261 --> 00:30:38.996
and play and everything they talked about.

00:30:38.996 --> 00:30:42.715
And I was almost crying, thinking,
my God, I'm so happy my son is here,

00:30:42.715 --> 00:30:44.007
this is such a privilege.

00:30:44.007 --> 00:30:47.970
So about two weeks into it,
I went to drop Logan off.

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:51.288
And the head teacher Sarah, who's
really tall in English pulled me over.

00:30:51.288 --> 00:30:54.403
She said Alison, Logan learned a new word.

00:30:54.403 --> 00:30:57.769
And I'm like great,
let's hear the brilliance.

00:30:57.769 --> 00:30:59.530
She goes, it's fuck.

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:03.804
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I go pale and I'm like, uh-huh, and

00:31:03.804 --> 00:31:07.589
she goes with any idea where
he may have heard that word?

00:31:07.589 --> 00:31:11.552
So I say well, I'm sure it's for me,
I said where else would he hear it?

00:31:11.552 --> 00:31:14.823
I said I don't know when and
where, but it was probably me.

00:31:14.823 --> 00:31:16.922
She's like it's okay, other kids curse but

00:31:16.922 --> 00:31:20.210
we just don't want you to
think they learned it here.

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.780
And don't worry about it,
and I said, no problem.

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.850
And I went back to my car and cried.

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:28.405
I'm like, I'm in the best school and
now they think I'm the worst mom,

00:31:28.405 --> 00:31:30.180
my kids cursing up a storm.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:33.308
So I go to pick them up later on and
Sarah calls me over,

00:31:33.308 --> 00:31:35.705
Allison, did you go to your car and cry?

00:31:35.705 --> 00:31:37.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:38.600
&gt;&gt; I said, yeah, I did.

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:40.770
And she's like Allison.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.529
So that's why we love you
because you're the only

00:31:43.529 --> 00:31:45.865
parent who has ever admitted it was them.

00:31:45.865 --> 00:31:49.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This happens all the time.

00:31:49.430 --> 00:31:53.660
She says, we trust you, you're so
endearing and we became so close.

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:57.313
And I ended up doing the auction thing and
the annual fun, but

00:31:57.313 --> 00:32:00.170
that was where my trust was built.

00:32:00.170 --> 00:32:02.790
And Sarah's still a dear friend.

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:06.060
So the more transparent you are,
the more people trust you.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.730
We know this from the trust radar and
reputation management.

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:11.890
There's shame in hiding the truth.

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:16.850
It's much bigger burden on you to try to
hide who you are and what you've done.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:21.770
Own it your candor will inspire
others to also be honest.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.715
All right don't be afraid
to share your truth.

00:32:24.715 --> 00:32:27.788
I was at Qualtrics a few
years ago before COVID.

00:32:27.788 --> 00:32:32.639
And I got to see three of my heroes
Oprah and Sir Richard Branson and Obama.

00:32:32.639 --> 00:32:37.264
And then this gentleman came out to speak,
his name was Billy McDermott,

00:32:37.264 --> 00:32:39.661
CEO of SAP, I didn't know who he was.

00:32:39.661 --> 00:32:44.329
I was in an audience of 11,000 people,
so I'm sure I wasn't alone, but

00:32:44.329 --> 00:32:49.470
he came out very good looking guy, salt
and pepper hair, but wearing sunglasses.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:53.773
And he sat down in this dark auditorium,
and started talking to Andy the Moderator.

00:32:53.773 --> 00:32:57.370
And all I could think of is,
why is he wearing sunglasses?

00:32:57.370 --> 00:32:58.460
Is he too cool for us?

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:00.250
Is he Jack Nicholson?

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:01.580
What's he trying to say?

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:05.859
Because I'm always looking at signals and
I'm so distracted by his glasses that I do

00:33:05.859 --> 00:33:09.535
something I rarely do, which is I
pulled up my phone, which no one should

00:33:09.535 --> 00:33:12.370
ever do when they're in an auditorium
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:13.383
&gt;&gt; And I look at my phone and

00:33:13.383 --> 00:33:14.580
I look them up.

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:16.530
And sure enough, he's had an accident.

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:21.418
He was walking down the stairs and he
tripped and he was holding a glass and it

00:33:21.418 --> 00:33:27.130
shattered and a shard of glass went into
his eye and he had all these surgeries.

00:33:27.130 --> 00:33:29.660
So what I was thinking was if I had
coached him, I would have said,

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:31.750
look Billy,
you've got to know your audience.

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:35.200
You've 11,000 people 10%
won't know who you are.

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:36.900
And they're going to make assumptions.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:40.534
So before you start talking you say, hey,
I just want to let you know, I had some

00:33:40.534 --> 00:33:44.600
surgeries on my eye I need to wear
glasses, I'm not giving you any shade.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:45.860
And then, you go on.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:49.420
And then all of a sudden,
you feel empathy, you feel closer,

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:50.660
you feel connected.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:56.123
And you're not distracted from
the reason he's actually there.

00:33:56.123 --> 00:33:58.984
So the lesson is, say who you are,

00:33:58.984 --> 00:34:04.520
share your experiences be authentic and
don't make excuses.

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:07.660
Don't be embarrassed for who you are,
your stories tell a tale and

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:09.240
they help people connect to you.

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:12.750
So why should you create a narrative?

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:14.680
I know you've heard me
say this many times.

00:34:14.680 --> 00:34:16.740
It's more interesting
to start with a story.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:18.840
People can carry your story.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:21.670
It helps you connect and
it's you at your most authentic.

00:34:22.710 --> 00:34:24.836
What makes you stand out in the crowd?

00:34:24.836 --> 00:34:26.500
You're all brilliant here.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.030
What makes you stand out?

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:31.241
Each of you has something
that makes you stand out.

00:34:31.241 --> 00:34:34.695
So you have to figure out
your differentiating factor,

00:34:34.695 --> 00:34:39.610
your secret sauce is what I like
to call it, your zone of genius.

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:42.119
What do people again,
tell you about yourself?

00:34:42.119 --> 00:34:44.803
All of you who've done the pee rap, right?

00:34:44.803 --> 00:34:45.406
My god.
So

00:34:45.406 --> 00:34:47.749
I apologize to everyone had
to take the surveys, but

00:34:47.749 --> 00:34:49.940
you learned what do people say about you?

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:54.128
When you figure out your secret sauce and
superpower you need to leverage it.

00:34:56.023 --> 00:34:58.030
So number nine, it's not what happens.

00:34:58.030 --> 00:34:59.470
It's how you deal with it.

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:02.423
Reputation management started that way,
it is my mantra,

00:35:02.423 --> 00:35:04.328
it will probably go on my gravestone.

00:35:04.328 --> 00:35:09.735
Shit happens, it's going to happen,
how are you dealing with it?

00:35:09.735 --> 00:35:14.722
How you deal with it says way more
about you, than what actually happened.

00:35:14.722 --> 00:35:19.448
So, what if you have a reputation for
being unapproachable, sarcastic,

00:35:19.448 --> 00:35:24.098
arrogant, late, blaming others,
being too quiet and not speaking up,

00:35:24.098 --> 00:35:25.910
not joining in activities.

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:29.820
Well, you can behave your
way to a new reputation.

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:34.244
This is from Joe Peterson when he
guested in my class, but an example for

00:35:34.244 --> 00:35:39.890
you is let's say you know someone,
you're someone who is habitually late.

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:44.803
And it's kind of funny and it's quirky,
and people think this person but

00:35:44.803 --> 00:35:48.756
what it's saying is your time
isn't as valuable as my time is.

00:35:48.756 --> 00:35:54.900
I don't respect your time and
after a while it's not cute anymore.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:59.698
So what I said to someone as I said
you can change this reputation by

00:35:59.698 --> 00:36:01.803
being consistently early.

00:36:01.803 --> 00:36:03.461
So get to the next meeting early, and

00:36:03.461 --> 00:36:07.223
everyone's going to say, what are you
doing here early, you're always late?

00:36:07.223 --> 00:36:08.860
And you'll say, no, no,
I'm going to be early for now.

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:11.854
And they're like, okay, then you show
up the next meeting and you're early,

00:36:11.854 --> 00:36:14.540
like, you're still doing the early thing.

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:16.200
And you'll yep,
still doing the early thing.

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:19.280
And then you keep showing up early and
early.

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:23.860
And all of a sudden that old reputation
of being late has now been put to bed.

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:26.827
And you don't even have a reputation for
it anymore.

00:36:26.827 --> 00:36:31.220
What I love about this example is
you're not stuck in a reputation.

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:36.128
You can always be fluid and tweak
something that you think you could do

00:36:36.128 --> 00:36:41.726
better and then consistently show up and
you will create that new reputation.

00:36:41.726 --> 00:36:46.740
I really believe in choosing to
engage in difficult conversations.

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:51.469
I think many of us would rather sweep it
under the rug or hope it passes by but

00:36:51.469 --> 00:36:54.826
from my experience people
hold on to these things and

00:36:54.826 --> 00:36:57.217
that becomes a fixed bias about you.

00:36:57.217 --> 00:37:01.906
And so have those hard conversations
because I don't think you want to leave

00:37:01.906 --> 00:37:06.235
things unsaid and especially as you
are getting ready to leave here.

00:37:06.235 --> 00:37:10.693
If there's anybody who you feel you
have a misunderstanding with, and

00:37:10.693 --> 00:37:15.160
you think,
it's too late to confront that, it's not.

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:19.532
I called up Amy, that producer from the
Home Shopping Network last year, after not

00:37:19.532 --> 00:37:24.440
speaking to her for over 20 years and
say can we talk about what happened?

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:26.250
And we talked about and
she apologized to me.

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:30.334
And it was a hard conversation that
I avoided for a very long time, but

00:37:30.334 --> 00:37:31.290
it really healed me.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.280
It also teaches you how to
verbalize complex thoughts.

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:39.634
You should be able to have
difficult conversations at work and

00:37:39.634 --> 00:37:44.670
in your personal life and not be
intimidated by the emotions involved.

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:47.776
It teaches you how to advocate for
yourself.

00:37:47.776 --> 00:37:52.577
I was really hurt by this but
I want to hear what you were thinking.

00:37:52.577 --> 00:37:57.349
It also creates trust because people
will know that you're an honest,

00:37:57.349 --> 00:37:58.790
forthright person.

00:38:00.870 --> 00:38:03.430
And, it helps you control
your own narrative.

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:07.300
So, it's not what you say,
it's how you say it.

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:08.790
I think I said this earlier.

00:38:08.790 --> 00:38:10.650
You can say anything to anyone.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:14.842
If you say it in a way that is kind,
compassionate and straightforward and

00:38:14.842 --> 00:38:15.391
candid.

00:38:15.391 --> 00:38:18.920
I do believe you can spin
anything into a positive.

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:21.875
And so let's say you got fired and
you're embarrassed and

00:38:21.875 --> 00:38:23.840
you don't want to talk about this.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:24.980
Well, you have two ways of doing it.

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:28.286
One you can say, this job is terrible,
like someone who's interviewing you and

00:38:28.286 --> 00:38:30.290
says, why were you only there for
six months?

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:34.565
You say, well, I was promised this and
it never happened, and they were terrible.

00:38:34.565 --> 00:38:36.730
[LAUGH] What does that say about you?

00:38:36.730 --> 00:38:39.561
How about you say,
you know what when I went to that job,

00:38:39.561 --> 00:38:43.750
I thought I was going to be doing this and
this, and it didn't work out that way.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:47.695
But in the six months I was there,
I learned this, this and this and

00:38:47.695 --> 00:38:50.529
now I'm even more prepared
to be here with you.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:55.182
So how you message something
tells people about you but

00:38:55.182 --> 00:39:00.151
you can put a positive spin on anything
even if it's not the greatest experience.

00:39:00.151 --> 00:39:04.498
I've had jobs that I had to squeeze
into a round peg into a square hole.

00:39:04.498 --> 00:39:07.180
And, that doesn't really
make sense [LAUGH].

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:09.100
But you know what?

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.310
I would always try to
find one thing of value.

00:39:11.310 --> 00:39:13.610
Maybe meeting a new person,
or a mentor, or a new skill.

00:39:14.820 --> 00:39:17.900
So again, we're back to,
if not you then who?

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:20.890
And I want you to opt in and
figure it out.

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:24.900
Okay, all of us have opportunities
that are presented to us but

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:28.466
many times we say it's not us,
I'm not ready for that.

00:39:28.466 --> 00:39:32.518
And that's because it's scary to opt in,
or we're feeling lost in life and

00:39:32.518 --> 00:39:34.559
we don't want to take anything new on.

00:39:34.559 --> 00:39:37.459
Or we're doing what we're doing
to make others happy, but

00:39:37.459 --> 00:39:40.560
we're not thinking about
what we really want.

00:39:40.560 --> 00:39:43.611
We're afraid to take a risk,
we're not feeling authentic, and

00:39:43.611 --> 00:39:45.190
we have imposter syndrome.

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:49.133
But I'm going to remind you that when
you're asked to opt in to do something,

00:39:49.133 --> 00:39:51.584
the deliverable is never
five minutes later.

00:39:51.584 --> 00:39:54.316
So if someone says I want
you to do this project, or

00:39:54.316 --> 00:39:58.544
I want you to get this promotion, or I
want you to go on this offsite and you're

00:39:58.544 --> 00:40:03.050
like, [SOUND] just think okay, say yes,
thank you, and then you go break it down.

00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:07.342
And you can research it, ask questions,
fill in the knowledge gaps.

00:40:07.342 --> 00:40:10.618
But you are all sitting here,
the most capable human beings,

00:40:10.618 --> 00:40:13.760
you're here because you
know how to do this.

00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:16.678
So just take those
opportunities when they come,

00:40:16.678 --> 00:40:20.670
don't get all that negative
self talk in your head.

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:24.061
And say what, I'll figure it,
out I'm smart enough to figure it out.

00:40:24.061 --> 00:40:26.532
So use your origin story
to create your narrative,

00:40:26.532 --> 00:40:28.720
you're all thinking what's my narrative?

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:31.781
Well you go back to your origin story,
a lot of this, forgive

00:40:31.781 --> 00:40:35.324
me if you this is going to be redundant
I'm going to go through it quickly but

00:40:35.324 --> 00:40:36.722
some of you don't know it.

00:40:36.722 --> 00:40:40.275
So using my own experience I went to Penn,
I interned at TV stations,

00:40:40.275 --> 00:40:44.260
I started a Good Morning America three
days later I worked there eight years,

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:47.615
I went to the shopping network,
I got on air, I sold barbecues.

00:40:47.615 --> 00:40:50.965
[LAUGH] And after that,
I went into the control room and

00:40:50.965 --> 00:40:53.582
I led the whole show which I really loved.

00:40:53.582 --> 00:40:58.220
And then I was wooed away to another
shopping network where I was the executive

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:01.280
producer, 16 hours of live television.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:06.283
I was the face of the network,
I created shows, hired and

00:41:06.283 --> 00:41:11.405
fired, and then this was me doing a promo,
let's see it.

00:41:11.405 --> 00:41:15.450
Hi, I'm Allison Kluger, your host for
designers corner here at GSN.

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.344
We get New York City designers
to bring in their jewelry, and

00:41:18.344 --> 00:41:20.530
all the jewelry is like a piece of art.

00:41:20.530 --> 00:41:23.397
Each one is handcrafted,
semi precious stones, unique design and

00:41:23.397 --> 00:41:24.865
you can't find it anywhere else.

00:41:24.865 --> 00:41:27.869
You don't have to wander around
the streets downtown looking for

00:41:27.869 --> 00:41:29.567
that very special piece of jewelry.

00:41:29.567 --> 00:41:31.370
We're going to bring it to you.

00:41:31.370 --> 00:41:34.479
We're going to bring you the piece of
jewelry that's beautiful, interesting,

00:41:34.479 --> 00:41:37.150
that you can't find anywhere else
with the price that you can afford.

00:41:37.150 --> 00:41:40.440
So be sure to catch me here at GSN,
the smart shoppers channel.

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:43.774
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah.

00:41:43.774 --> 00:41:46.410
[LAUGH] Someone had to do it, right?

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:49.847
And you don't want to
wander around looking for

00:41:49.847 --> 00:41:54.950
that very special piece of jewellery,
let me do it for you.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:59.174
Anyway, then I was wooed over by the view,
Barbara Walters was my idol,

00:41:59.174 --> 00:42:03.280
I had known her when I was an intern and
I always wanted to work with her.

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:06.119
I know that she's old and
you probably don't know her but

00:42:06.119 --> 00:42:09.320
she was a pioneer, and
I was like the thrill of my life.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:12.553
And then my husband went to the GSB and

00:42:12.553 --> 00:42:16.111
I had to move to here and
be an SO or an SOB.

00:42:16.111 --> 00:42:20.955
And I had to support us and go into,
I started a .com for women,

00:42:20.955 --> 00:42:24.770
I was a.com disaster, open TV snap me up.

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:26.316
I was an executive producer there.

00:42:26.316 --> 00:42:30.090
Then when I finished, I did a year with Al
Gore and I helped him set up current TV.

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:32.256
I took that job just to work with him,
not for

00:42:32.256 --> 00:42:35.230
the money cause believe
me there was no money.

00:42:35.230 --> 00:42:38.355
And then, after seven I'm sorry,
nine in Vitros and

00:42:38.355 --> 00:42:41.626
three years of trying,
I had trouble and more trouble.

00:42:41.626 --> 00:42:44.550
And I took seven years off to raise them.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:48.359
And I did start my own media consulting
business, but it was far and few between

00:42:48.359 --> 00:42:52.240
the amount of times I actually worked,
because I was their sole caretaker.

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:54.206
My husband was starting up companies.

00:42:54.206 --> 00:42:57.548
And then I came to this place,
which is a very trite photo, but

00:42:57.548 --> 00:42:59.720
it really says what I felt.

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:03.949
And a lot of you might be there right now,
or in 10 years you might be there.

00:43:03.949 --> 00:43:07.340
And I just had to say,
what's my narrative, right?

00:43:07.340 --> 00:43:09.792
So I figured these are the things
I'm really good at.

00:43:09.792 --> 00:43:14.020
And no matter where I go and what I do,
I'm going to bring this to it.

00:43:14.020 --> 00:43:15.802
So don't be afraid to repackage yourself.

00:43:15.802 --> 00:43:17.860
There are times you're
going to be pivoting.

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:20.539
There are times you're
going to recreate yourself.

00:43:20.539 --> 00:43:24.610
But everything you need is there,
you just have to cherry pick it out.

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:29.800
So here's the exercise that I teach
in project U and strategic pivoting.

00:43:29.800 --> 00:43:32.968
So again, many of you know it,
but for those who don't,

00:43:32.968 --> 00:43:34.626
it's called cake and icing.

00:43:34.626 --> 00:43:38.896
I visualize this exercise from this book,
and cake is what you really do well,

00:43:38.896 --> 00:43:42.440
it's your rational value,
it's your functional skills.

00:43:42.440 --> 00:43:45.880
Your icing is what people say about
you and how they describe you.

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:48.472
And together they make your strong brand,

00:43:48.472 --> 00:43:51.143
but they also enable
you to tell your story.

00:43:51.143 --> 00:43:54.678
So now with all those jobs I
told you about listed up here,

00:43:54.678 --> 00:43:58.140
what I do is I write down my
cake underneath each one.

00:43:58.140 --> 00:43:59.303
What are the talents?

00:43:59.303 --> 00:44:02.320
What's my functional benefits I can do?

00:44:02.320 --> 00:44:04.688
And then you see what keeps popping up.

00:44:04.688 --> 00:44:07.936
Well, I always create content,

00:44:07.936 --> 00:44:12.808
I'm innovative, oops, I'm problem solving,

00:44:12.808 --> 00:44:18.400
I coach talent, and
I'm an expert at messaging.

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:22.114
Look how many times that pops
up in my weird timeline, right?

00:44:22.114 --> 00:44:23.262
And then my icing.

00:44:23.262 --> 00:44:27.646
This is what people would say about me,
and if you don't know those words you ask

00:44:27.646 --> 00:44:31.160
your friends,
do a survey I really recommend surveys.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:34.530
And so these are the things that I
also deliver, what people think.

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:38.692
Now, if I were to create a narrative I
might go to each one of these steps and

00:44:38.692 --> 00:44:42.386
pick out an anecdote or
story under each one that shows my skills,

00:44:42.386 --> 00:44:46.940
that proves my credibility,
that shows a failure and a triumph.

00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:51.409
And that is how you can curate stories for
your narrative.

00:44:51.409 --> 00:44:54.800
So present the solution,
not the suggestion.

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:55.968
I see I'm running out of time.

00:44:55.968 --> 00:44:57.490
I'm going to have to be super fast.

00:44:57.490 --> 00:44:59.970
How many people know the Tyra
story before I go into it?

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:01.140
Show of hands.

00:45:01.140 --> 00:45:03.010
Okay, I have to tell it that,
I'm going to be really quick.

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:07.484
Tyra came here on SMX, I was in
the front row, I watched all her shows.

00:45:07.484 --> 00:45:09.942
And she talked about personal branding.

00:45:09.942 --> 00:45:14.080
And she was so amazing and
talked about her roles changing.

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:16.857
And I'm sitting here going,
I gotta teach with her.

00:45:16.857 --> 00:45:19.890
And I force my way backstage
with no permission.

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:25.550
And I have a 32nd narrative, and I say,
hi Tyra, I'm Allison Kluger, I teach here.

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:28.032
I was a TV producer at the view
in Good Morning America, and

00:45:28.032 --> 00:45:29.980
I know you've been on both shows.

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:32.668
I want to teach a course with you on
personal branding, a compressed course,

00:45:32.668 --> 00:45:34.150
what do you think?

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:35.198
And she was, I don't know.

00:45:35.198 --> 00:45:37.090
And I'm like, no, I have a great idea.

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:39.440
And I said,
I learned more from you in one hour.

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:42.845
And she's like, okay, and she gives me
her card and we make an appointment for

00:45:42.845 --> 00:45:43.880
a week later.

00:45:43.880 --> 00:45:47.065
In that week I go back and
I start creating the whole course,

00:45:47.065 --> 00:45:48.250
all six classes.

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:49.920
What are the textbooks
that I want to read?

00:45:49.920 --> 00:45:51.420
Who are going to be the guests?

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:52.973
What's every segment about?

00:45:52.973 --> 00:45:56.310
I'm going to stream it live on
Facebook to be differentiated.

00:45:56.310 --> 00:45:59.060
I'm going to come out of a live
TV station for the final project.

00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:00.800
We'll do visual branding.

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:03.479
And I put it all together with all
the compressed course dates that

00:46:03.479 --> 00:46:06.789
were available, and then I talked to her
again for a few hours, and I'm like, so

00:46:06.789 --> 00:46:09.010
here's the outline,
what do you want to do?

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:10.082
And she was amazing.

00:46:10.082 --> 00:46:12.832
She was, I can do this, and
I can do that, and blah blah blah, and

00:46:12.832 --> 00:46:15.740
she was the most brilliant
marketer I have ever met.

00:46:15.740 --> 00:46:18.030
And she made it easy and said,
okay, this is all great.

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:21.446
And I said, give me the two weeks you can
do and you have to be here every day.

00:46:21.446 --> 00:46:22.437
And she's like, okay.

00:46:22.437 --> 00:46:23.860
And give me a backup, and she's okay.

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:26.422
Then I went and
redid the whole proposal, and

00:46:26.422 --> 00:46:30.075
then I anticipated what all the push
back would be from the dean.

00:46:30.075 --> 00:46:33.154
And then I thought about how
I'm going to handle all that.

00:46:33.154 --> 00:46:37.375
And then I went to the assistant
dean of the Business School.

00:46:37.375 --> 00:46:40.449
And I said, I want to teach a course
with Tyra Banks, she's a business woman,

00:46:40.449 --> 00:46:43.903
here's her proof, this is the course, this
is how it's going to benefit our students,

00:46:43.903 --> 00:46:46.553
this is how it's going to be
different from all the other courses,

00:46:46.553 --> 00:46:50.145
these are the takeaways, these are out
textbooks, these are our guests.

00:46:50.145 --> 00:46:52.045
And he asked me every
question I anticipated,

00:46:52.045 --> 00:46:55.225
starting with Tyra is not our brand,
I don't think she's the right person.

00:46:55.225 --> 00:46:56.682
I'm like, she is.

00:46:56.682 --> 00:46:58.880
[LAUGH] And he said yes.

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:04.540
So, the lesson here is that
I would say no there she is.

00:47:04.540 --> 00:47:05.933
I'm going to go back quickly.

00:47:05.933 --> 00:47:08.660
Present the solution, not the suggestion.

00:47:08.660 --> 00:47:13.016
And the reason I say this is that if I
had gone the day after Tyra was here and

00:47:13.016 --> 00:47:14.803
went to the assistant dean and

00:47:14.803 --> 00:47:20.090
said, I think I want to teach with Tyra I
have this idea, he would have said no.

00:47:20.090 --> 00:47:22.776
Absolutely not and
I would have been dead in the water and

00:47:22.776 --> 00:47:24.650
that would have been it for me.

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:29.138
So if you want to create change for
yourself, don't complain about things,

00:47:29.138 --> 00:47:31.220
don't identify the problems.

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:33.948
Create the solutions,
just go to an interview and say,

00:47:33.948 --> 00:47:38.410
I've looked at your website and you're
missing this, and I'd love to take over.

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:42.015
And I want to be in charge and this is
the budget, but this is just a tip for

00:47:42.015 --> 00:47:45.950
those of you who aren't as bold or
need more time or introverted.

00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:49.390
But it's worked for me a lot and
it's probably my number one tip, and

00:47:49.390 --> 00:47:50.690
then I did it with Alex.

00:47:50.690 --> 00:47:53.229
I created the course ahead of time.

00:47:53.229 --> 00:47:57.850
Alex was available to guests speak,
I wasn't sure they'd let him, but

00:47:57.850 --> 00:48:02.270
the course now exists and
that's what we did, that's one of my tip.

00:48:02.270 --> 00:48:06.644
So I'm telling you to be authentic you and
I'm telling you all these things you need

00:48:06.644 --> 00:48:10.310
to do that might push you out
of your comfort zone, right.

00:48:10.310 --> 00:48:16.040
So finally, my last story is I was
crying a Good Morning America.

00:48:16.040 --> 00:48:19.350
I was about two months in,
I was almost 21.

00:48:19.350 --> 00:48:24.130
And this woman, Sonia Selby, right, an
English producer, I was scared of her too.

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:26.730
She pulled me in her office and
she shut the door and said, Allison,

00:48:26.730 --> 00:48:28.610
I don't ever want to see you crying.

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:29.560
Do you know why?

00:48:29.560 --> 00:48:30.610
And I was like.

00:48:30.610 --> 00:48:33.660
And intuitively I knew I but
I knew she was going to tell me.

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.742
And she says, because you're young,
you're female and you're attractive and

00:48:36.742 --> 00:48:37.980
no one is going to trust you.

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:39.672
No one is going to give
you responsibility and

00:48:39.672 --> 00:48:42.110
no one's going to think you're reliable.

00:48:42.110 --> 00:48:44.956
She goes, if you're having a bad day,
you come in my office and

00:48:44.956 --> 00:48:46.150
you can close the door.

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:47.904
But if someone says to you, how are you?

00:48:47.904 --> 00:48:48.680
You say I'm great.

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:50.110
Do you understand?

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:50.610
I said, yes.

00:48:50.610 --> 00:48:52.350
And she was, get outta here.

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:56.882
And so she threw me out of the office and
of course, what did I want to do, cry, but

00:48:56.882 --> 00:48:59.404
I didn't, but this was an epiphany for me.

00:48:59.404 --> 00:49:03.308
And this was how I became the leader I
want to be, I wanted to be reliable,

00:49:03.308 --> 00:49:07.110
I wanted to be respected,
I wanted to be given the hard work.

00:49:07.110 --> 00:49:08.690
And so I became unflappable.

00:49:08.690 --> 00:49:11.177
And they'd say go to Alison
she can take care of her.

00:49:11.177 --> 00:49:12.939
Go to Alison she's really
easy to work with.

00:49:12.939 --> 00:49:14.840
Go to Alison, she's a problem solver.

00:49:14.840 --> 00:49:17.152
And then I went to jobs
that I was on air and

00:49:17.152 --> 00:49:20.360
in the control room where
anything could go wrong.

00:49:20.360 --> 00:49:23.977
So by showing up every day,
the way I wanted to show up,

00:49:23.977 --> 00:49:26.259
is how I created the reputation and

00:49:26.259 --> 00:49:30.976
how I became the leader I want to be,
even though I was a very shy kid, and

00:49:30.976 --> 00:49:36.060
obviously I cry a lot, I still do but,
that's how I showed my fear.

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:40.330
So, act like the leader you want to be and
it becomes authentic.

00:49:40.330 --> 00:49:41.800
Everyone can do it.

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:44.890
You're not cheating yourself
out of your true personality.

00:49:44.890 --> 00:49:49.117
These are the leaders who I want to be
like Barbara Walters for her work ethic,

00:49:49.117 --> 00:49:52.499
Oprah for transparency,
Obama for integrity and empathy.

00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:56.933
Michelle Obama inspirational and
powerful and then of course,

00:49:56.933 --> 00:49:59.440
Ruth Bader Ginsburg for breaking barriers.

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:03.540
Those were my North Stars,
figure out who yours are.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:06.500
So, what are my takeaways for today?

00:50:06.500 --> 00:50:11.236
Lead with love, compassion over
aggression, when down, do for others,

00:50:11.236 --> 00:50:15.156
storytelling is a secret weapon,
differentiate yourself.

00:50:15.156 --> 00:50:20.072
Choose to engage in those hard
conversations, spin it into a positive,

00:50:20.072 --> 00:50:25.072
present the solution not the suggestion,
opt in and figure it out later,

00:50:25.072 --> 00:50:27.900
and act like the leader you want to be.

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:31.966
Because, if not you then who?

00:50:31.966 --> 00:50:39.682
Thank you

00:50:39.682 --> 00:50:49.682
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:50:51.245 --> 00:50:57.440
&gt;&gt; Kendy [APPLAUSE]

00:50:57.440 --> 00:50:59.715
&gt;&gt; Thank you everybody.

00:50:59.715 --> 00:51:00.770
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Allison.

00:51:00.770 --> 00:51:02.207
I think we have time for one or
two quick questions, and

00:51:02.207 --> 00:51:04.029
then you can probably catch
Allison outside for any follow up.

00:51:04.029 --> 00:51:04.756
&gt;&gt; My God, all right..

00:51:04.756 --> 00:51:05.988
&gt;&gt; For any follow up.

00:51:05.988 --> 00:51:10.120
&gt;&gt; I'm excited, not nervous for
those questions I don't know.

00:51:10.120 --> 00:51:11.400
Who's got a questions to stone me?

00:51:13.120 --> 00:51:14.180
Yes.

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:17.590
&gt;&gt; The mic is actually right behind
you if you want in the cup holder.

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:18.380
&gt;&gt; How convenient.

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:19.500
Did you plant that person?

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:20.290
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:51:20.290 --> 00:51:20.970
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:51:20.970 --> 00:51:21.860
&gt;&gt; Hi, Alison.

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:23.220
Thank you so much.

00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:28.483
So I think one of the greatest gifts from
coming to the GSB is just the platform

00:51:28.483 --> 00:51:33.770
it gives us to share a message or
just a cause that we really care about.

00:51:33.770 --> 00:51:36.525
I love to hear how you think about
when you choose to step in and

00:51:36.525 --> 00:51:39.130
out of the public eye,
and how you manage that.

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:43.446
And also to that, how you stay authentic,
when so much of what we're pitching is

00:51:43.446 --> 00:51:47.899
sometimes to our loved ones and to our
masses, and sometimes feels inconsistent.

00:51:47.899 --> 00:51:49.490
How do we close that gap?

00:51:49.490 --> 00:51:50.920
&gt;&gt; Thank you for that question.

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:54.770
I don't always choose
sometimes I'm chosen.

00:51:54.770 --> 00:51:56.690
And then I have to opt in.

00:51:56.690 --> 00:52:01.477
And so I would say that I've had impostor
syndrome probably my entire first 45

00:52:01.477 --> 00:52:02.560
years of my life.

00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:04.676
I'm only 30.

00:52:04.676 --> 00:52:05.780
So I don't really know how that

00:52:05.780 --> 00:52:07.570
math works, but
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:07.570 --> 00:52:09.563
&gt;&gt; But, many times I've been tapped on

00:52:09.563 --> 00:52:13.869
the shoulder to do something, or someone's
come to me and said, Alison we want

00:52:13.869 --> 00:52:18.142
you to be on air, and I will battle with
the fear and I'll force myself to do it.

00:52:18.142 --> 00:52:22.160
And by the way, the reality is never
as scary as what you think it is.

00:52:22.160 --> 00:52:23.940
We build it up in our heads.

00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:27.119
So the outward facing me is
just from challenging myself.

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:31.402
When I started teaching here
I had another epiphany, and

00:52:31.402 --> 00:52:34.026
my epiphany is I'm going to
say yes to everything.

00:52:34.026 --> 00:52:37.755
At this point in my career
I know what I'm good at.

00:52:37.755 --> 00:52:41.386
I worked really hard to create
a course here and if I can't do it,

00:52:41.386 --> 00:52:44.967
I'm going to say I can't do it or
I'm going to find someone who can.

00:52:44.967 --> 00:52:49.527
So in being authentic about it, I just
I'm very clear what I'm not good at,

00:52:49.527 --> 00:52:52.094
we all know I'm not
going to do accounting and

00:52:52.094 --> 00:52:55.895
budgeting but I know what I'm
really good at and I lean into it.

00:52:55.895 --> 00:52:57.933
I hope that answers your question.

00:52:57.933 --> 00:53:00.702
Yes.

00:53:00.702 --> 00:53:01.770
&gt;&gt; Chris is right behind you.

00:53:01.770 --> 00:53:02.868
Perfect.

00:53:02.868 --> 00:53:05.194
&gt;&gt; Hi,
&gt;&gt; Hi, Miriam.

00:53:05.194 --> 00:53:06.576
&gt;&gt; Hi [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Glad you made it,

00:53:06.576 --> 00:53:07.231
&gt;&gt; That was amazing.

00:53:07.231 --> 00:53:07.838
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:53:07.838 --> 00:53:09.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks for reminding me.

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:12.403
My questions about your
slides on transparency and

00:53:12.403 --> 00:53:14.630
being infront upfront with people.

00:53:14.630 --> 00:53:19.461
I'm just wondering Is that always the case
or has it been time where it's better to

00:53:19.461 --> 00:53:23.330
not speak up or say something on
your mind in a certain situation?

00:53:23.330 --> 00:53:24.440
&gt;&gt; I've grown into that as well.

00:53:24.440 --> 00:53:26.000
And thank you for
that great question, Miriam.

00:53:27.110 --> 00:53:31.966
I'm a great believer that the more you
are transparent or have radical candor

00:53:31.966 --> 00:53:36.790
is people in reputation management
know the gift you're giving people.

00:53:36.790 --> 00:53:40.210
They don't have to work so
hard to figure you out.

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:44.403
And I've always said, some of my students
are actually shy or introverted and yet

00:53:44.403 --> 00:53:48.353
when they did the reputation survey,
people would say, you seem arrogant or

00:53:48.353 --> 00:53:50.670
rude so you don't care about me.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:52.851
And I say that's because you're shy and
you're quiet, but

00:53:52.851 --> 00:53:54.391
people are making assumptions about you,

00:53:54.391 --> 00:53:58.305
because they just want to be friends with
you, and they think you're rejecting them.

00:53:58.305 --> 00:54:02.310
So, sometimes it's good to say,
I'm not the most social person but

00:54:02.310 --> 00:54:04.047
I'm really happy to be here.

00:54:04.047 --> 00:54:07.977
But front load the information you want,
just like Billy McDermott.

00:54:07.977 --> 00:54:10.533
There are different
shades of transparency,

00:54:10.533 --> 00:54:13.450
if it's going to diminish
your power in a job.

00:54:13.450 --> 00:54:16.829
Then I might find a better
way to say something but

00:54:16.829 --> 00:54:20.299
I always believe that
eventually the truth outs.

00:54:20.299 --> 00:54:25.630
And if you're working so hard to be
you're not it's gonnabe exhausting.

00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:29.666
And I think ultimately we all want to
be accepted and appreciated for

00:54:29.666 --> 00:54:32.344
who we truely are and
what we bring to table.

00:54:32.344 --> 00:54:35.890
And for the things we feel insecure about,
we can ask for help.

00:54:35.890 --> 00:54:39.590
But if you don't broadcast and say,
you know what, I'm not so good with this.

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:42.390
I need some help.

00:54:42.390 --> 00:54:43.780
Everyone wants to help you.

00:54:43.780 --> 00:54:45.370
So I hope that answered your question.

00:54:45.370 --> 00:54:48.523
I think that transparency
is a personal thing.

00:54:48.523 --> 00:54:51.256
But I'm a pretty open book,
and it's worked well for

00:54:51.256 --> 00:54:53.380
me except when I called Amy a bitch.

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:55.800
That was not good but I learned from that.

00:54:57.050 --> 00:54:58.610
Thank you for the question.

00:54:58.610 --> 00:54:59.460
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Alison.

00:54:59.460 --> 00:55:01.135
&gt;&gt; Thank you all.

00:55:01.135 --> 00:55:02.350
[APPLAUSE] I love you all.

